    still reaches nobody with the browser closed.

  I'd recommend starting in-app only, with the delivery layer written as a dispatcher behind an interface so email is a
  second implementation rather than a retrofit. In-app is the substrate every other channel reads from anyway — you need
  the notification records regardless — and it's the only piece that's useless to defer. Adding email later is additive
  if the dispatcher seam exists from day one; that seam is cheap now and awkward to introduce afterward.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Which delivery channels should this cover? → In-app feed (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m [1mNext: what counts as a change.[0m One caveat before you pick — the task model is currently [3mnothing[0m, so every event type
  you choose is also a field or feature I have to build for tasks first. Status, assignee, and due date are cheap
  (columns on the task). [1mComments are not[0m — that's a second table, a UI, and its own permissions, roughly doubling the
  task-side work before any notification fires. Worth it if discussion is central to how you imagine this app; worth
  deferring if you mostly care about "someone moved my task."

  Everything here is per-event-type, so adding a type later is additive — this choice is cheap to revise, unlike the
  three before it.

  Two things I'll fold into the design rather than ask about, since they're near-universal: you never get notified about
  your own actions, and rapid repeat edits to the same task collapse into one notification rather than a stream.

